  <section id="daily-cleaning" class="content-section">
    <h2>Daily Cleaning Routines</h2>
    <p>
      A few simple daily habits can make household cleaning easier.
      Making beds, wiping kitchen surfaces, washing dishes, putting
      belongings away, and tidying frequently used rooms can help
      prevent clutter and dirt from accumulating.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rather than allowing small tasks to build up, establish a
      manageable routine that fits comfortably into your day.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="weekly-cleaning" class="content-section">
    <h2>Weekly Cleaning</h2>
    <p>
      Weekly cleaning provides an opportunity to give the home
      more thorough attention. Tasks may include vacuuming,
      mopping floors, cleaning bathrooms, changing bedding,
      dusting furniture, and emptying household bins.
    </p>
    <p>
      Dividing these responsibilities across several days can
      make the workload more manageable.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="deep-cleaning" class="content-section">
    <h2>Deep Cleaning</h2>
    <p>
      Deep cleaning focuses on areas that are not always included
      in everyday routines. This may involve cleaning behind
      furniture, washing baseboards, cleaning cupboard interiors,
      attending to vents, and addressing accumulated dirt in
      less frequently used spaces.
    </p>
    <p>
      Plan deeper cleaning periodically according to the needs
      and condition of your home.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="kitchen-cleaning" class="content-section">
    <h2>Kitchen Cleaning</h2>
    <p>
      Kitchens require regular cleaning because food preparation
      and daily activity can quickly create spills, crumbs, and
      residue. Keep work surfaces clean, wash dishes promptly,
      clean appliance exteriors, and pay attention to sinks,
      taps, cupboard handles, and floors.
    </p>
    <p>
      Follow manufacturer instructions when cleaning ovens,
      refrigerators, dishwashers, and other appliances. Use
      food-safe cleaning practices on food-contact surfaces.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="bathroom-cleaning" class="content-section">
    <h2>Bathroom Cleaning</h2>
    <p>
      Regular bathroom care helps maintain clean and comfortable
      facilities. Clean sinks, taps, toilets, showers, bathtubs,
      mirrors, and frequently touched surfaces. Allow appropriate
      ventilation to help manage moisture.
    </p>
    <p>
      Follow product directions and use suitable cleaning
      materials for tiles, grout, glass, and bathroom fixtures.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="floors-surfaces" class="content-section">
    <h2>Floors & Household Surfaces</h2>
    <p>
      Different flooring and surface materials require different
      cleaning methods. Hardwood, laminate, tile, stone, vinyl,
      and carpet should be cared for according to their
      manufacturer's recommendations.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use appropriate cleaning products, avoid excessive moisture
      on sensitive materials, and test unfamiliar products in
      an inconspicuous area first.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="windows-glass" class="content-section">
    <h2>Windows & Glass</h2>
    <p>
      Clean windows and mirrors can make a home feel brighter
      and more inviting. Use appropriate glass-cleaning products
      and clean cloths to remove fingerprints, dust, and
      everyday marks.
    </p>
    <p>
      Choose suitable weather conditions for exterior window
      cleaning and use safe equipment when working at height.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="dusting" class="content-section">
    <h2>Dusting & Indoor Care</h2>
    <p>
      Dust can accumulate on furniture, shelving, decorative
      objects, electronics, and other household surfaces.
      Regular dusting helps maintain a tidy interior.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use appropriate cloths and cleaning methods for delicate
      finishes, electronics, artwork, and other sensitive items.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cleaning-products" class="content-section">
    <h2>Cleaning Products & Tools</h2>
    <p>
      Having the right equipment can make household cleaning
      more efficient. Common supplies include microfiber cloths,
      brushes, sponges, mops, vacuum cleaners, buckets, and
      suitable surface-cleaning products.
    </p>
    <p>
      Always read product labels, follow safety instructions,
      keep cleaning supplies in their original containers,
      and store them securely away from children and pets.
      Never mix cleaning chemicals, particularly bleach with
      ammonia or acidic cleaners.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="stain-removal" class="content-section">
    <h2>Stain Removal</h2>
    <p>
      Prompt attention can make many household stains easier
      to address. Different materials and stains require
      different treatment methods, so identify the fabric
      or surface before choosing a cleaning approach.
    </p>
    <p>
      Blot rather than rub many fresh fabric spills, check
      care labels, and test cleaning products in an
      inconspicuous area before wider application.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="seasonal-cleaning" class="content-section">
    <h2>Seasonal Cleaning</h2>
    <p>
      Seasonal cleaning offers an opportunity to refresh the
      home and prepare for changing weather. Spring may be
      a time for windows, cupboards, and general refreshing.
      Autumn can be useful for organizing storage, cleaning
      entryways, and preparing the home for colder weather.
    </p>
    <p>
      Create a seasonal checklist to spread larger tasks
      throughout the year.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="home-maintenance" class="content-section">
    <h2>Home Maintenance</h2>
    <p>
      Regular household maintenance can help identify small
      problems before they become larger concerns. Check
      visible plumbing, inspect weather stripping, clean
      accessible vents, monitor caulking, and keep outdoor
      entryways in good condition.
    </p>
    <p>
      Follow manufacturer maintenance recommendations and
      contact qualified professionals for electrical,
      structural, plumbing, or other specialized repairs.
    </p>
  </section>

